  "worked_solution": "First, let's represent $D$ as a type II region. \n\n- To determine the $y$-limits, we need to find the $y$-coordinates of the points at which the curves intersect. So, we set the following equation and solve it for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2-y & =\\frac{y}{2} \\\\ -\\frac{3}{2}y & =-2 \\\\ y & =\\frac{4}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $y$-limits are \n$0\\le y\\le \\frac{4}{3}.$\n\n- To determine the left and right functions, we draw a horizontal arrow from left to right through the region, as usual.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14055472/assets/e6c9332f8f59e2561af9ba5aa89311263fb772dc987ed0b15d91a6d5eea5cf8e.png)\n\nAs a result, we get\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le \\frac{4}{3},\\,\\frac{y}{2}\\le x\\le 2-y}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\u222c}_{D}xy\\,dA & ={\\int }_{0}^{4/3}{\\int }_{y/2}^{2-y}xy\\,dx\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{4/3}[{\\int }_{y/2}^{2-y}xy\\,dx]dy\\end{aligned}\n$$",
